// Practice quiz: Method overloading
// Formative, low-stakes retention practice. Titles name the objective id each item practises.

$CATEGORY: java-foundations/methods-and-recursion/method-overloading

::o1 valid overloads::Which pair of methods in one class is a valid overload of total? {
=total(int a, int b) and total(int a, int b, int c)#Correct. They share a name and differ in the number of parameters.
~total(int a, int b) and total(int x, int y)#Not quite. Parameter names are not part of the signature, so both are total(int, int).
~int total(int a) and long total(int a)#Not quite. They differ only in return type, which does not make an overload.
~total(int a) declared twice, once static and once not#Not quite. Adding or removing static does not change the parameter list.
}

::o1 changing the list::A class has print(String s, int n). Which added method is a new overload? {
=print(int n, String s)#Correct. The parameter types appear in a different order, so the parameter list differs.
~print(String text, int count)#Not quite. Renaming parameters leaves the types, and so the signature, unchanged.
~int print(String s, int n)#Not quite. A different return type with the same parameter list is a duplicate, not an overload.
~private print(String s, int n)#Not quite. Changing access does not change the parameter list.
}

::o2 most specific widening::A class has f(long x) and f(double x). Which one does the call f(3) run? {
=f(long x), because both fit by widening and long is the more specific type#Correct. An int widens to long and to double; a long can itself widen to double, so f(long) is more specific.
~f(double x), because double is the widest type#Not quite. javac prefers the most specific applicable method, not the widest.
~Neither: the call does not compile because no method takes an int#Not quite. An argument may be widened to fit a parameter.
~The call is ambiguous#Not quite. It would be ambiguous only if neither method were more specific than the other.
}

::o2 char argument::A class has g(int x) and g(double x). Which one does g('A') run? {
=g(int x), because a char widens to int#Correct. char widens to int, and int is more specific than double.
~g(double x), because a char is not a number#Not quite. A char widens to int and to double; javac picks the more specific int.
~Neither: a char needs a g(char x) method#Not quite. An exact match is not required when widening works.
~Whichever g is declared first#Not quite. Declaration order never chooses an overload.
}

::o3 return type only::One class declares boolean check(int n) and void check(int n). What happens when it is compiled? {
=javac reports that check(int) is already defined, because the return type is not part of the signature#Correct. Both methods have the signature check(int), so the second is a duplicate.
~It compiles, and calls that use the result pick the boolean version#Not quite. javac chooses a method from its name and argument types, not from how the result is used.
~It compiles, and the method declared last wins#Not quite. Two methods with the same signature in one class are an error.
~It compiles, but calling check fails at run time#Not quite. The duplicate is a compile-time error, so nothing runs.
}

::o3 what resolves a call::When javac decides which overload a call runs, which information does it use? {
=The method name and the types of the arguments#Correct. That is why two methods that differ only in return type cannot be told apart.
~The return type the caller expects#Not quite. A call can ignore the result, so the return type cannot decide it.
~The name of the variable that stores the result#Not quite. Assignment targets play no part in choosing an overload.
~The order in which the methods are declared#Not quite. Declaration order never matters for overload choice.
}
